Changes requested at exact head 522a54c7801590e0aa266f2cf8032ce0b77d0d8c. Inspected the entire 617-line note, its 371-line predecessor plus all revision-2 changes, and the cited contract/protocol, MCP and CLI sources at base bd520fb9. The only Git change is this note. The widened question in assert ae96bba1 is included. No source implementation, deployment or adoption is authorized by this verdict.

1. P1 — The proposed human review principal cannot review. Section 2 row 5 and D7 say this project's policy uses role:checker for reviews. R-GEN-5 explicitly allows a checker only check, note and delegate/undelegate; policy cannot widen this. Such a member receives an impossible review duty and submission is refused role-forbids. The current gitseq actor named checker is not the Artroom Role checker. Use an authorized maintainer/member/agent (or admin) member/team for human reviewer duties and keep the machine service's checker identity separate. Correct the section 1 signer table to include admins. Source: docs/protocol.md R-GEN-5 and R-OBL-2; packages/contract/src/roster.ts Role.

2. P2 — Section 6.2 and the summary/6.4 assert soundness using states that are not terminal. A successful landing marks a generation landed but leaves the lane held with its lease; release then leaves it unheld and reclaimable. packages/contract/src/lanes.ts defines only held and unheld, and packages/mcp/src/tools.ts tells callers to release or renew after landing. The retained live run also lands before a separate release. packages/contract/src/landing.ts explicitly excludes unresolved from LandTerminal and includes it in SlotHolding: admin attention names debt, it does not complete the operation. Policy may refuse release on an ordinary lane; R-LANE-8 expiry is a separate transition. Acceptance examples for individual acts under different configurations do not establish that every transition is live in the same net under arbitrary policy. The paper definition requires a defined net and reachability from each reachable marking, not a named actor who might progress: https://michaelblondin.com/papers/BMO22a.pdf section 2.2. Revise this to a proposed abstraction with explicit completion boundary (generation/operation vs reusable lane), policy and provider/fairness assumptions, and obligations/lease/cleanup/publication accounted for separately. Remove the established-sound-net and safe-freeze deductions unless supported by that model. A model-checking spike may remain future work; this review does not demand it to adopt an honestly limited argument.

3. P2 — The conversation mapping is partial in more ways than counter, so amendment 5 is not shown to complete a homomorphism. The note's first table allows requester A to withdraw after B has promised; its amendment permits filer withdrawal only while unheld, so that transition disappears after takeover. Completion in the conversation is declared by A, whereas holder B may land when policy obligations are met without any obligation requiring A's acceptance. Decline ends the A-to-B conversation, but the proposed decline re-addresses an open item to the filer; that can be a sound application choice, but distinguish termination of one conversation from survival of the work item. Requesting alternative conditions and re-addressing a performer are also different operations. Winograd's original account distinguishes reporting by B, satisfaction by A, and backing out after commitment: https://www.lri.fr/~mbl/ENS/CSCW/2016/papers/Winograd-HCI87.pdf pp. 8–9. Provide an explicit state/actor/condition mapping and list lost transitions or the additional decisions needed to preserve them. Treat policy-mediated satisfaction as an explicit choice. The artifact lifecycle is a defensible starting point for code delivery; it does not establish completeness for general commitments, investigations, questions, or decisions completed without a main change.

4. P2 — The permission and governance inventory misstates two authority boundaries. Section 1.2 says admins sign rotate-recovery, but R-GEN-4 permits only the recovery key. D2 treats sole-admin-self-approval as applying to the proposed notes/docs owner review; R-ADMIN-2 is the exception for the platform's obl_admin-approval on .artroom/**. A custom owner-review obligation on notes/** does not gain that flag or exemption: packages/room/src/admission.ts adds it only when ADMIN_APPROVAL is present. Explicitly choose the custom rule's independence/allowSelf behavior within R-OBL-2, and distinguish an adopted pack from this note's proposal. D3's landApproval: role:admin is not an existing policy schema field (packages/contract/src/policy.ts and packages/policy/src/validate.ts); express the gate through actual require/land rules or label the notation as pseudocode.

5. P2 — Remove the invented note-anchor gap. Section 2 row 6 and D5 overlook the envelope target. NoteAnchor already contains act or lane/generation/head/path/line/endLine; Envelope uses it as the note target; RoomApi.note takes it; MCP note exposes anchor; CLI accepts LANE#GENERATION:PATH:LINE. This is implemented on main, including head validation in packages/room/src/admission.ts note(). NoteBody omitting an anchor does not mean the whole envelope omits it. No new field or deferred amendment is needed.

6. P2 — The freeze is based on an incomplete inventory despite the request requiring every verb's fields. Section 1 omits ReclaimBody (expectedGeneration, optional lease/goal/plan/because), all act target variants, exact CheckBody volatile/ok/detail/landOp, most RosterOp fields, all signed-request envelope/body fields, and the inputs for both current and planned MCP reads. CLI commands are named without their options/input differences; packages/cli/src/cli.ts is only the entrypoint, while main.ts implements them. Supply a compact, typed inventory with exact file/rule citations for target and body variants, the 10/14/18 tool stages, requests and CLI inputs. Distinguish field additions and proposed changes from what is frozen now; the literal claim that shapes never changed contradicts the additive fields described in 1.7. Amendment 5 must identify which ReclaimBody/ReleaseBody variants permit no lease and who can close/re-address, with a takeover-versus-withdraw/re-address race and attention resolution in its acceptance map. This can stay concise; avoid reproducing unrelated contract records.

7. P2 — Correct the Gerrit comparison before using uniqueness as justification. Gerrit project.config is versioned in refs/meta/config in the same repository and contains access rights, labels and submit requirements; configuration changes can themselves be reviewed, and submit requirements can apply to refs/meta/config. Label copyCondition is a configurable predicate, not merely a trivial-rebase heuristic. Sources checked on 2026-10-02: https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/config-project-config.html ; https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/config-submit-requirements.html ; https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/config-labels.html . Artroom's same-main policy activation and signed, replayable authority are useful differences, but “rules in the repository under governance” is not unique. Cite and qualify the remaining product placements rather than infer absent capabilities from memory. Lease-by-scope also permits overlap under by-scope; explain awareness vs enforced exclusivity separately.

8. P2 — Distinguish the jam application's musical workflow from development of the artroom-jam repository. Section 5 concludes that the jam can self-host now without D1 because a musical session has no backlog. The migration tracked by 7a3bbf8f is the jam's development first; the jam note section 9 explicitly places its requests, proposals and reviews in Artroom once self-hostable. That development has the same work-tracking question as platform development. State whether it waits for D1 or accepts the claim/release practice and its costs, subject to the existing readiness decision. The jam note section 9 also already offers validators as reviewers across accounts, so the final paragraph's mandatory checker deployment change is not the only zero-amendment route. No migration is proven ready by a vocabulary review or the narrow fixture run.

Architecture: the small vocabulary, immutable proposal generations, typed evidence and room-owned publication are a useful foundation. The note currently collapses work-item, conversation, lane, generation and publication lifecycles and overstates the resulting completeness; the revised argument must preserve those distinct states and their coupling.
Security: role admission, recovery-key custody, custom review independence, filer/performer closure authority and service-bound machine-check authority must be described precisely. P1 is an unusable review policy; the other authority claims would lead to incorrect administrative expectations.
Simplification: remove the nonexistent D5 amendment, use actual policy syntax, replace unsupported proof/uniqueness language with a small explicit state-and-authority mapping, and keep recommendations separate from adopted decisions. This strengthens the proposed materialization without cutting functionality or using schedules to justify omissions.

Validation: documentation-only exact-head inspection and primary-source checking; no runtime tests are warranted for this note. The live candidate 09d4 is reviewed separately and its passing gates do not validate these design conclusions.